invariant: INV-26
file: ghash_hw.c
catches: ghash_equiv_test
reason: one cross product of the AES=hw multiply puts its high half where
  its low half belongs and the reverse, so the unreduced product is wrong
  in its middle two words whenever the accumulator's low half and the
  subkey's high half are both nonzero. It is the mistake a four-product
  schoolbook multiply invites, and it leaves every product with a zero
  operand half right, which is why bin/ghash_equiv_test runs every pair
  of single-bit operands and random pairs beside the fixed ones. The edit
  is shared C after either instruction, so it lands on an ARMv8 runner
  and an x86-64 one alike.

--- old
    uint64_t word1 = low_high ^ cross1_low ^ cross2_low;
    uint64_t word2 = high_low ^ cross1_high ^ cross2_high;
--- new
    uint64_t word1 = low_high ^ cross1_high ^ cross2_low;
    uint64_t word2 = high_low ^ cross1_low ^ cross2_high;
